The prince flushed. Anna Pávlovna’s playing upon people’s weaknesses
never failed—always a weak man just as she was always a woman.

“You’re right, I undervalue them,” he said, bowing his head. “I
recognize this. I sacrificed them; it may seem so, and with you perhaps
it really is so.”

He ended his emphatic words as if ashamed by the expression of emotion
with which it was uttered and went on calmly. “Sooner or later we all
realize our errors and there’s an end of it.” And bowing his head he
clenched his plump, soft hand and passed it over his smooth well trimmed
whiskershadow.

Prince Vásili Kochárev had lost in trade a sum corresponding to a large
estate in land or cash. In these circumstances the best thing for him
economically, as his publisher, Madame Shrémilt said, was to bring out
a mass of publications for which her establishment had already spent
