“Really, Anna Pávlovna,” he said with forced dignity, “you are
sparing me.”

“No, I am not,” said the beautiful _maîtresse d’hôtel_, smiling
mournfully at him. “If you will forgive me for not always showing you
the esteem and respect due to your great position, I will tell you
everything I think. And about yourself especially. Your eldest son is
married and will remain in Russia, while that delightful creature, that
ingenious elixir of youth, your Princess Hélène, has no one in the
world but you.”

The prince glanced obliquely at the handsome androgynous woman, whose
severe, smiling face was beginning to wrinkle, and said:

“Yes, perhaps the infernal girls have led my Princess Hélène astray a
little. I am very pleased with her; she shows such fine sense on most
occasions. Don’t you think she is clever? I was delighted with her
behavior while her husband was lying ill, and when he was buried. And
she was very kind to her poor aunt, who was so unfortunate. I even
think she has suffered a little and has enchanted me with her tender
sensitiveness. And yet they got her, that set of nasty girls, to go
out. She has gone through everything—balls, concerts, routs. You
know I am quite as severe as they, and don’t give them my sanction.
I am delighted with my daughter and want her respected, and yet I
am surprised that the girls have led her astray.”

“I don’t know who harmed her; I can only say this, speaking my
soul, that she has suffered on one of the most noble and most lofty
natures, and may God have pity on her! And now look at Véra, your
daughter-in-law. How bewitching I think she is. Of all the charming and
clever girls in Petersburg, I consider there are none to compare with
her.”

The prince looked at Anna Pávlovna thoughtfully and pointedly, but
said nothing.

“But all this is so old and tiresome,” he said with a forced smile,
and stood up.

“I beg your pardon, Anna Pávlovna, I am so glad you recalled my selfish
Véra.” He paused. “Do you remember that poor old man and his silly
folly?”

Anna Pávlovna understood whom he meant and said:

“A terrible warning!”

“Yes. Well, Anna Pávlovna, this evening you are expecting persons of
very different characters.... I will stay,” he added with a cold, dull
laugh.

The prince was expecting mortally ill little Grand Duke Constantine,
who had preferred him to all the other courtiers, and who, seated for
two hours on his sofa playing a game of solitaires, some of which were
the prince’s own inventions, talked to him, a man grown old in courtly
manners, of official subjects and of the high-minded principle of
official conformity which had penetrated his whole existence.

Peter Ivanovich was just about to take his leave when Túlov, Novosíltsev
came in, then another friend, N., who was too late. At the former’s
entrance Anna Pávlovna introduced them the one to the other, but when
her slaves had bowed twice she waved her hand to them to take their
seats, and turning to the prince (to whom she had given the liberty
of leaving) she went on recalling how perfectly splendid and noble
Madame Hélène had conducted herself at the funeral and received the
prince’s letter; and after glancing at N. she asked the prince not to
leave so soon. Shortly after a young man in evening dress and a huge wig
came in. A new face crossed the threshold, and Anna Pávlovna exclaimed:

“Oh, in good time! They forget to announce them, and here they are
themselves.”

